            "Modern: Frequently played with local house rules like 'low-hole' wild cards.",
          ],
          evolution: 'The game evolved into several regional distinct variants: Mexican Stud (5-card), Mississippi Mud (7-card discard), and Dakota (Wild Card Hi-Lo).',
          cultural: "Typically a 'kitchen table' or 'social club' game rather than a casino tournament standard, prized for the tension of the simultaneous 'reveal' phase.",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        setup: {
          players: '2-7 players (limit based on 52-card deck exhaustion).',
          deck: 'Standard 52-card deck.',
          equipment: 'Standard cards, poker chips, dealer button.',
          dealing: "Varies by variant. Standard 'Roll Your Own' deals 3 cards down initially, then 4 more sequentially.",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Form the best 5-card poker hand out of 7 dealt cards to win the pot.',
          gameplay: "1. Ante/Blinds. 2. Deal 3 cards face-down. 3. Players simultaneously 'roll' (reveal) one card. 4. Betting round. 5. Deal 4th card down; players reveal one of their hidden cards. 6. Repeat for 5th and 6th cards. 7. Deal 7th card down. 8. Final betting round and Showdown.",
          keyRules: [
            "Reveals must be simultaneous (usually on a count of 'one-two-three-flip').",
            'You cannot look at the card you just received before choosing which one to flip (in strict variants).',
            'Once a card is rolled, it remains face-up for the duration of the hand.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Keep your best cards (Aces, potential pair partners) hidden as long as possible. Reveal cards that make you look weak to induce bluffs, or reveal scary cards to push people out.',
          intermediate: "In Dakota, 'buying the option' is essential if you have a powerful wild card (like an Ace as your low-hole) and don't want to risk a Deuce or Three 'replacing' it as the wild rank.",
          advanced: "Information suppression: if you roll a Jack and have another Jack hidden, you are 'representing' a pair but keeping the strength concealed. If you roll two disparate cards, you look like you are chasing a flush/straight.",
          tips: [
            "Observe which cards opponents choose to reveal first; cautious players reveal their lowest 'junk' cards.",
            'In Hi-Lo variants, always track the number of low cards showing to judge the safety of a low-hand split.',
            'The choice of what to reveal is your primary weapon - use it to tell a story.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
